Jimbo S. Harris (jimbo@statsci.com) wrote:

[deletia]
: The complexity of nethack is such that, unlike moria or rogue, you *can't*
: just sit down and expect to do well, or even close. The spoilers serve, IMHO,
: to make the game *playable*, not "too easy". I think they should be cleaned
: up and packaged with the sources.

I think that's the strongest point of Nethack: you just can't expect to 
lose your interest by doing too well in the first few games, let alone 
winning it! At first you might descend to the fifth level of the dungeon 
only to discover that you were too weak or the monsters were too strong.

Time passes and you have learnt from your mistakes. The first 
confrontation with a mumak is fatal -- again you bite the dust. Next 
time the mumak dies, but you get disintegrated by a breath of a dragon... 
Enough to put down the most persistent nethacker? Oh no! "Progress, 
progress! We are in the beginning of a new era, where the most important 
thing is the development of your equipment!" 8-)

I didn't like Larn at all, because in the very first game I managed to 
get decent equipment, quite high an experience level and a few spells for 
good measure. I was strolling merrily along a corridor and out of nowhere 
came an invisible whatchacallem-devil-thingy and killed me.

Nethack, on the other hand, had me killed in the first dungeon level, by 
a goblin! And still does every now then. I hope you didn't miss the point 
here...

Including the spoilers in the Nethack package is IMHO a bad idea. You 
should be able to play the game successfully without getting a couple of 
wishes in the beginning stages for GDSM and SoR. With a lot of practice, 
that is. The spoilers indeed spoil your game, they should be available if 
you want them, but not in the standard set-up.

